WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:07.606
♪ ♪

00:00:07.606 --> 00:00:10.810
ALEX: When I was a kid,
I didn't do very good in school,

00:00:10.810 --> 00:00:12.845
and, uh, my dad would
always try to,

00:00:12.845 --> 00:00:15.348
I guess, get me thinking about
my future, what I wanted to do.

00:00:15.348 --> 00:00:16.416
You know,
he would just ask me, like,

00:00:16.416 --> 00:00:21.354
"What do you wanna be
when you grow up," you know?

00:00:21.354 --> 00:00:25.357
I always told him that
I wanted to be a caveman.

00:00:25.357 --> 00:00:27.693
I didn't wanna be an engineer.

00:00:27.693 --> 00:00:29.995
I didn't wanna be a doctor.

00:00:29.995 --> 00:00:31.664
Didn't wanna be an astronaut.

00:00:31.664 --> 00:00:33.933
I wanted to be a caveman.

00:00:36.802 --> 00:00:39.972
So making a fire here

00:00:39.972 --> 00:00:43.910
in this extremely
primitive shelter,

00:00:43.910 --> 00:00:47.213
I kind of feel like
I'm living my childhood dream.

00:00:47.213 --> 00:00:51.818
♪ ♪

00:00:57.623 --> 00:00:59.725
Mother Nature is
setting the rules,

00:00:59.725 --> 00:01:03.562
and all I got to do is find
a way to play her game.

00:01:03.562 --> 00:01:06.365
♪ ♪

00:01:06.365 --> 00:01:13.105
♪ ♪

00:01:13.105 --> 00:01:14.874
I'm just getting packed up,
ready to go.

00:01:14.874 --> 00:01:18.378
I got some food,
stuff to cook with,

00:01:18.378 --> 00:01:19.813
fire-starting stuff.

00:01:19.813 --> 00:01:21.548
Just got enough essentials
to keep me comfortable

00:01:21.548 --> 00:01:22.782
while I'm there.

00:01:22.782 --> 00:01:24.450
Well, to keep me alive
while I'm there.

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:26.019
I wouldn't say comfortable.

00:01:26.019 --> 00:01:28.587
This will be my first night
in the primitive shelter,

00:01:28.587 --> 00:01:32.825
and I don't know
how cold it'll get.

00:01:32.825 --> 00:01:35.728
I'm headed out to go
beaver hunting today.

00:01:35.728 --> 00:01:37.930
I've never hunted them before.

00:01:37.930 --> 00:01:40.800
The reason I specifically
am targeting beavers

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:42.735
is because they're basically
the largest animal

00:01:42.735 --> 00:01:46.839
that's legal for me to hunt
this time of year.

00:01:46.839 --> 00:01:48.240
It's a pretty fatty animal,

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:51.544
so it's a pretty,
pretty rich calorie source.

00:01:55.348 --> 00:01:57.216
My hunting cabin is basically
at the halfway point

00:01:57.216 --> 00:02:00.919
between my old cabin
and the hunting grounds.

00:02:00.919 --> 00:02:04.991
It basically is just a place
I can stop, get dry, get warm,

00:02:04.991 --> 00:02:08.495
cook a dinner,
and not freeze to death.

00:02:10.229 --> 00:02:16.101
♪ ♪

00:02:16.101 --> 00:02:17.469
KALEB: We can just
walk out our door

00:02:17.469 --> 00:02:19.906
and we're out
in the great outdoors,

00:02:19.906 --> 00:02:21.474
enjoying nature,

00:02:21.474 --> 00:02:26.212
breathing fresh, clean,
non-polluted air.

00:02:26.212 --> 00:02:28.548
And I think that's just
a great way to live life.

00:02:30.483 --> 00:02:35.321
♪ ♪

00:02:35.321 --> 00:02:36.823
You can follow me.

00:02:36.823 --> 00:02:38.424
BRITTANY: Okay.

00:02:38.424 --> 00:02:41.794
 NARRATOR: Isolated in the small
 community of McCarthy, Alaska,

00:02:41.794 --> 00:02:44.663
 Brittany and Kaleb Rowland
 have chosen to raise their kids

00:02:44.663 --> 00:02:48.501
 away from the hustle
 of a major city.

00:02:48.501 --> 00:02:50.402
 But with limited stores
 in town,

00:02:50.402 --> 00:02:54.340
 they'll need to travel over 300
 miles along a dangerous highway

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:57.343
 to get much-needed
 building supplies.

00:02:57.343 --> 00:02:59.445
 And in order to make
 the most of their trip,

00:02:59.445 --> 00:03:02.148
 they never leave
 with an empty trailer.

00:03:06.019 --> 00:03:07.252
KALEB: Brittany and I
are gonna be heading over

00:03:07.252 --> 00:03:10.689
to this junk vehicle that's
sitting out on the river bar.

00:03:10.689 --> 00:03:12.491
And we're gonna load it up
on the trailer

00:03:12.491 --> 00:03:13.826
and haul it into town.

00:03:13.826 --> 00:03:16.062
BRITTANY: Over the last couple
years, one of Kaleb's goals

00:03:16.062 --> 00:03:19.998
has been to get all of the old
clunkers out of McCarthy.

00:03:19.998 --> 00:03:23.903
So I drive the forklift while
Kaleb does the heavy lifting

00:03:23.903 --> 00:03:25.872
and any of the dirty stuff.

00:03:28.174 --> 00:03:29.943
KALEB: My turn.

00:03:33.479 --> 00:03:35.481
I'm gonna pick up
that yellow vehicle there

00:03:35.481 --> 00:03:38.785
and load it on the trailer
and strap it down,

00:03:38.785 --> 00:03:41.554
and we'll get out of here.

00:03:41.554 --> 00:03:44.524
BRITTANY: Kaleb does this
several times a year.

00:03:44.524 --> 00:03:47.460
It's really an act of love
for the community.

00:03:49.962 --> 00:03:50.929
KALEB: We live in the middle

00:03:50.929 --> 00:03:53.132
of the world's largest
national park,

00:03:53.132 --> 00:03:54.567
and people come out here

00:03:54.567 --> 00:03:57.236
and pay thousands
and thousands of dollars

00:03:57.236 --> 00:03:59.238
to see the pristine wilderness,

00:03:59.238 --> 00:04:03.676
and junk vehicles really
don't need to be a part of that.

00:04:03.676 --> 00:04:06.111
And so I try to get them off
to the recycling center

00:04:06.111 --> 00:04:07.446
so that they will, hopefully,

00:04:07.446 --> 00:04:10.716
someday be turned
into a new vehicle again.

00:04:10.716 --> 00:04:12.751
♪ ♪

00:04:12.751 --> 00:04:17.924
♪ ♪

00:04:17.924 --> 00:04:19.091
Looks like the clouds
are rolling in.

00:04:19.091 --> 00:04:20.292
The weather might be changing,

00:04:20.292 --> 00:04:23.596
so we need to get the heck out
of here to get to Anchorage.

00:04:23.596 --> 00:04:25.264
The road is pretty tricky.

00:04:25.264 --> 00:04:26.598
We're getting into wintertime.

00:04:26.598 --> 00:04:30.969
There's ice, potholes,
road glaciers, animals.

00:04:30.969 --> 00:04:33.706
You have to have
your wits about you.

00:04:33.706 --> 00:04:34.874
BRITTANY: Think it'll hold?

00:04:34.874 --> 00:04:37.910
KALEB: Looking good. I think
that'll stay on my trailer.

00:04:37.910 --> 00:04:38.945
Let's hit the road.

00:04:38.945 --> 00:04:40.213
BRITTANY: Cool.

00:04:44.716 --> 00:04:46.419
MICHAEL: If you don't
challenge yourself,

00:04:46.419 --> 00:04:49.788
if you don't push
out of your comfort zone,

00:04:49.788 --> 00:04:52.425
you're not gonna grow
to the extent that you could.

00:04:54.593 --> 00:04:56.462
All these sleds that I have,

00:04:56.462 --> 00:04:59.865
they should be turned
into flower pots.

00:04:59.865 --> 00:05:02.735
Most of my sleds are breaking,
and they're all these,

00:05:02.735 --> 00:05:05.671
these traditional
cordage-lashed,

00:05:05.671 --> 00:05:09.642
mortise-and-tenon joint
kind of sleds.

00:05:09.642 --> 00:05:11.444
That thing is done.

00:05:15.047 --> 00:05:17.416
My bread-and-butter sled.

00:05:17.416 --> 00:05:21.320
I've hauled a lot of fish and
lot of caribou with this sled.

00:05:21.320 --> 00:05:22.688
And it's worked hard.

00:05:22.688 --> 00:05:25.657
It's been, it's been worn out.

00:05:25.657 --> 00:05:27.126
But it's time to, to build one

00:05:27.126 --> 00:05:29.628
that will hold up
to these malamutes.

00:05:29.628 --> 00:05:31.264
They destroy everything.

00:05:35.033 --> 00:05:38.470
A half a decade
of running these dogs...

00:05:38.470 --> 00:05:40.306
(bleep)

00:05:40.306 --> 00:05:43.909
...I realize that
they're just totally brutal

00:05:43.909 --> 00:05:45.511
on whatever I hook to them.

00:05:45.511 --> 00:05:46.445
Hey, hey, hey, hey!

00:05:46.445 --> 00:05:48.880
The force that they exert
is just too much

00:05:48.880 --> 00:05:51.684
for the typical way
that sleds are built.

00:05:53.519 --> 00:05:56.622
So now it's time to take matters
into my own hands

00:05:56.622 --> 00:05:59.392
and build a good sled.

00:05:59.392 --> 00:06:03.896
Most sleds have been designed
exclusively for sport, racing.

00:06:03.896 --> 00:06:06.399
And that kind of stuff,
that doesn't fly in my world,

00:06:06.399 --> 00:06:08.333
because I got work to do.

00:06:08.333 --> 00:06:11.504
Hunting, fishing,
hauling firewood.

00:06:14.874 --> 00:06:15.907
So this is a first.

00:06:15.907 --> 00:06:18.344
This is the first sled
I will have ever built,

00:06:18.344 --> 00:06:21.346
and it has to pass
a lot of tests.

00:06:21.346 --> 00:06:24.216
These are the makings
of my runners.

00:06:24.216 --> 00:06:28.320
These are a little bit thicker
than quarter-inch-long strips.

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:31.123
They are 14 foot long.

00:06:31.123 --> 00:06:34.593
And it'll be a real flexible
runner, too, when I'm done.

00:06:34.593 --> 00:06:36.729
You want the sled
to give and flex.

00:06:36.729 --> 00:06:38.330
And if that thing
ain't flexible,

00:06:38.330 --> 00:06:40.699
if that thing ain't strong,

00:06:40.699 --> 00:06:44.069
that thing is gonna be
destroyed by these dogs.

00:06:44.069 --> 00:06:48.741
♪ ♪

00:06:48.741 --> 00:06:50.175
You know, as I'm putting
the sled together,

00:06:50.175 --> 00:06:52.345
I've been taking
a few little moments

00:06:52.345 --> 00:06:55.748
to pause and think about
the people I come from.

00:06:57.416 --> 00:07:00.452
And I was reading a book
that my grandmother was in

00:07:00.452 --> 00:07:03.522
called
 Our Lives Are in Our Hands,

00:07:03.522 --> 00:07:07.593
and she made a lot
of amazing things.

00:07:07.593 --> 00:07:11.397
Whether it was pack baskets,
fishing creels, axe handles,

00:07:11.397 --> 00:07:13.498
hockey sticks, snowshoes,

00:07:13.498 --> 00:07:18.203
she made really strong,
flexible stuff.

00:07:18.203 --> 00:07:21.206
And the book
 Our Lives Are in Our Hands

00:07:21.206 --> 00:07:22.874
got a lot of meaning for me

00:07:22.874 --> 00:07:26.345
as I continue on the tradition
of building traditional things,

00:07:26.345 --> 00:07:29.348
whether they're canoes
or whether they're dog sleds.

00:07:29.348 --> 00:07:32.217
It's really in my blood.

00:07:32.217 --> 00:07:37.189
♪ ♪

00:07:37.189 --> 00:07:40.559
Whoa, that's a big sled.

00:07:40.559 --> 00:07:43.429
This thing is
11 and a half foot long.

00:07:43.429 --> 00:07:45.297
This is a real freight sled.

00:07:45.297 --> 00:07:48.000
This thing is built to haul
a load of caribou,

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:51.337
firewood, fish, lots of gear
on an expedition.

00:07:51.337 --> 00:07:53.639
You name it,
this thing will swallow it.

00:07:53.639 --> 00:07:55.207
But it's super flexible.

00:07:55.207 --> 00:07:56.742
Even the runners.

00:07:56.742 --> 00:07:59.945
But this thing is done, and
I need to catch up on some rest,

00:07:59.945 --> 00:08:02.481
wake up in the morning,
hook these dogs up,

00:08:02.481 --> 00:08:06.185
and give this thing
a maiden voyage.

00:08:06.185 --> 00:08:13.191
♪ ♪

00:08:13.191 --> 00:08:16.595
JESSI: Self-reliance,
you know, it's freedom.

00:08:16.595 --> 00:08:18.164
That's what it is.

00:08:22.101 --> 00:08:24.136
♪ ♪

00:08:24.136 --> 00:08:30.175
♪ ♪

00:08:30.175 --> 00:08:36.648
♪ ♪

00:08:36.648 --> 00:08:37.683
(engine sputters)

00:08:38.950 --> 00:08:41.487
CHRIS: My throttle's all stuck.

00:08:41.487 --> 00:08:43.255
Hey, Jess, can you grab me
the blowtorch?

00:08:43.255 --> 00:08:46.191
I think my throttle's
all iced up.

00:08:46.191 --> 00:08:48.126
Machines are really
important out here.

00:08:48.126 --> 00:08:49.528
You got to take care of them.

00:08:49.528 --> 00:08:50.596
If you don't treat
your machine well,

00:08:50.596 --> 00:08:51.597
it's not gonna work well

00:08:51.597 --> 00:08:53.932
and it's not gonna make it
through the winter.

00:08:53.932 --> 00:08:55.101
JESSI: What's going on?

00:08:55.101 --> 00:08:57.770
CHRIS: Uh, the throttle's
all froze up on here.

00:08:57.770 --> 00:08:59.138
It's not moving.

00:09:00.605 --> 00:09:02.507
JESSI: Oh.
This freezing rain sucks.

00:09:02.507 --> 00:09:06.645
CHRIS: Yeah, I mean, I'm gonna
try to warm it up a little bit.

00:09:06.645 --> 00:09:08.948
See if I can just
get it to let go.

00:09:11.617 --> 00:09:13.018
Hopefully, I can just
thaw it out real quick

00:09:13.018 --> 00:09:15.153
and I don't have to disassemble
the whole thing.

00:09:15.153 --> 00:09:16.322
We got really short days
out here,

00:09:16.322 --> 00:09:17.789
and I don't wanna spend
half my day

00:09:17.789 --> 00:09:19.658
wrenching on my three-wheeler.

00:09:19.658 --> 00:09:22.027
You can see it's still frozen.

00:09:22.027 --> 00:09:24.764
So that means I got to dig
into it a little bit more.

00:09:28.267 --> 00:09:29.668
JESSI: You know, there wouldn't
be any ice in the line

00:09:29.668 --> 00:09:32.070
if we had a shed
to keep this in.

00:09:32.070 --> 00:09:35.007
CHRIS: Yeah.

00:09:35.007 --> 00:09:37.075
If this three-wheeler was
just stored inside of a shed,

00:09:37.075 --> 00:09:39.111
it wouldn't have got covered
in freezing rain,

00:09:39.111 --> 00:09:41.780
and I wouldn't be out here
spending my precious daylight

00:09:41.780 --> 00:09:43.749
wrenching on it again.

00:09:43.749 --> 00:09:46.552
I just got to get
this shed built.

00:09:46.552 --> 00:09:48.954
 NARRATOR: At their isolated,
 remote homestead,

00:09:48.954 --> 00:09:52.191
 Chris and Jessi Morse rely on
 what little equipment they have

00:09:52.191 --> 00:09:55.193
 in order to survive.

00:09:55.193 --> 00:09:57.029
 With darkness and cold coming,

00:09:57.029 --> 00:10:00.466
 it's crucial their machinery
 remains functional.

00:10:04.036 --> 00:10:05.971
JESSI: This is some icy stuff
up here, huh?

00:10:05.971 --> 00:10:08.874
CHRIS: Yeah, be careful.

00:10:08.874 --> 00:10:12.277
JESSI: Well, hi, Toz.
You don't seem to mind, do you?

00:10:12.277 --> 00:10:16.248
CHRIS: Alright, so let's see,
that's, that's almost six feet.

00:10:19.351 --> 00:10:21.553
Seven feet's probably
all we need.

00:10:21.553 --> 00:10:23.822
(chainsaw revving)

00:10:23.822 --> 00:10:28.660
♪ ♪

00:10:28.660 --> 00:10:32.731
I figure we need about ten logs
in total to build the shed.

00:10:32.731 --> 00:10:36.402
We'll do three or four at a time
until we get them all.

00:10:36.402 --> 00:10:37.870
Oof!

00:10:40.872 --> 00:10:42.908
Wow, that thing's heavy.
I don't know if I can move that.

00:10:42.908 --> 00:10:44.176
JESSI: We need to go back
and get the sled,

00:10:44.176 --> 00:10:46.112
and you put the front part
in the sled.

00:10:46.112 --> 00:10:48.981
CHRIS: Yeah. I can get that
small, black, skinny sled.

00:10:48.981 --> 00:10:50.915
JESSI: You wanna run back now
or you wanna try it?

00:10:50.915 --> 00:10:52.150
I mean, I can cut the next log.

00:10:52.150 --> 00:10:53.919
CHRIS: Yeah, cut it,
and I'll, I'll drag this.

00:10:53.919 --> 00:10:55.054
JESSI: Okay.

00:10:57.722 --> 00:10:59.692
(chainsaw revving)

00:11:02.260 --> 00:11:04.430
I have cut my fair share of wood

00:11:04.430 --> 00:11:08.968
in this exact area
with this exact saw.

00:11:08.968 --> 00:11:15.007
So, uh, pretty comfortable in
the wood yard with a chainsaw.

00:11:15.007 --> 00:11:17.376
(bleep)

00:11:17.376 --> 00:11:19.411
Got it stuck. (laughs)

00:11:19.411 --> 00:11:22.348
That's what I get for saying
I've done this before.

00:11:22.348 --> 00:11:24.784
(revving)

00:11:35.461 --> 00:11:36.896
(chainsaw stops)

00:11:38.163 --> 00:11:39.832
(bleep)

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:44.370
JESSI: I really don't
wanna break this.

00:11:46.838 --> 00:11:48.040
Damn.

00:11:48.040 --> 00:11:50.776
(laughs) Oh, my gosh!

00:11:52.311 --> 00:11:54.746
I got this.
I know what I'm doing.

00:11:54.746 --> 00:11:57.849
Ha! What a crock.

00:11:57.849 --> 00:12:01.954
♪ ♪

00:12:01.954 --> 00:12:08.693
♪ ♪

00:12:08.693 --> 00:12:11.497
So don't get mad.

00:12:11.497 --> 00:12:13.798
I got the chainsaw stuck.

00:12:13.798 --> 00:12:14.766
CHRIS: Hopefully not underneath.

00:12:14.766 --> 00:12:16.134
JESSI: Definitely underneath.

00:12:16.134 --> 00:12:18.971
CHRIS: We don't have
another one, so.

00:12:18.971 --> 00:12:21.307
JESSI: Can you just come over
here and give me a hand?

00:12:25.277 --> 00:12:27.045
I didn't wanna pinch up top.

00:12:27.045 --> 00:12:29.848
Anyways, doesn't matter.
I did it wrong.

00:12:29.848 --> 00:12:35.421
♪ ♪

00:12:37.255 --> 00:12:38.290
Thanks.

00:12:38.290 --> 00:12:41.460
So we had to lift and then pull,
and I just couldn't.

00:12:41.460 --> 00:12:43.528
Wonderful.

00:12:43.528 --> 00:12:44.429
CHRIS: We're really far
out here,

00:12:44.429 --> 00:12:46.665
we don't have neighbors
for miles and miles,

00:12:46.665 --> 00:12:48.233
so we really got
to work together

00:12:48.233 --> 00:12:50.936
and not get mad at each other
and work as a team.

00:12:54.573 --> 00:12:55.441
Nice.

00:12:55.441 --> 00:12:57.442
Yeah, I'm gonna go
load that log.

00:12:57.442 --> 00:13:00.345
Um, go ahead
and cut this one off.

00:13:00.345 --> 00:13:01.480
JESSI: Okay.

00:13:01.480 --> 00:13:02.881
CHRIS: And then just keep doing
exactly what you're doing,

00:13:02.881 --> 00:13:04.783
and I'll keep moving logs.

00:13:04.783 --> 00:13:05.785
JESSI: Okay.

00:13:08.353 --> 00:13:13.224
♪ ♪

00:13:13.224 --> 00:13:15.794
Chris and I are
a really good team.

00:13:15.794 --> 00:13:18.497
I'm just stoked for our project.

00:13:18.497 --> 00:13:19.832
CHRIS: I finally feel like
we're settling in

00:13:19.832 --> 00:13:22.200
and I'm getting the hang
of the work program out here,

00:13:22.200 --> 00:13:23.868
hauling water, hauling wood,

00:13:23.868 --> 00:13:27.272
working all day long till dark
and then working a little more.

00:13:27.272 --> 00:13:29.307
♪ ♪

00:13:29.307 --> 00:13:34.713
♪ ♪

00:13:34.713 --> 00:13:37.249
This is a chainsaw mill.

00:13:37.249 --> 00:13:40.819
This is my first time
using a log mill.

00:13:40.819 --> 00:13:42.354
I have planed
a lot of boards by hand,

00:13:42.354 --> 00:13:44.857
but they don't come out
very smooth.

00:13:44.857 --> 00:13:47.259
This device right here should
make my bar and my chain

00:13:47.259 --> 00:13:49.194
last a lot longer.

00:13:49.194 --> 00:13:53.231
♪ ♪

00:13:53.231 --> 00:13:59.271
♪ ♪

00:13:59.271 --> 00:14:04.709
♪ ♪

00:14:04.709 --> 00:14:11.516
♪ ♪

00:14:11.516 --> 00:14:14.619
Ha ha! Let's see what
it looks like, huh?

00:14:14.619 --> 00:14:16.454
JESSI: Maybe too thin, huh?

00:14:16.454 --> 00:14:17.589
CHRIS: Yeah.

00:14:17.589 --> 00:14:19.958
JESSI: I mean, but I, eee,
that's a little thin, love.

00:14:19.958 --> 00:14:21.059
I think a...

00:14:21.059 --> 00:14:22.227
CHRIS: A little bit thicker?

00:14:22.227 --> 00:14:24.463
JESSI: ...300-pound bear
will snap right through that.

00:14:24.463 --> 00:14:26.698
CHRIS: Yeah. You know.

00:14:26.698 --> 00:14:28.733
JESSI: One more time.

00:14:28.733 --> 00:14:33.405
♪ ♪

00:14:33.405 --> 00:14:37.108
(revving)

00:14:37.108 --> 00:14:37.909
CHRIS: I don't really know
what I'm doing.

00:14:37.909 --> 00:14:39.345
I'm not a carpenter
by any means.

00:14:39.345 --> 00:14:41.179
I've been a mechanic
and a welder my whole life,

00:14:41.179 --> 00:14:43.515
uh, so building with wood is
a little bit out of my element.

00:14:43.515 --> 00:14:46.118
I'm learning and
making things work better.

00:14:48.086 --> 00:14:49.254
Alright.

00:14:49.254 --> 00:14:50.522
Now, that's a nice board.

00:14:50.522 --> 00:14:54.893
JESSI: That's our first
perfect board!

00:14:54.893 --> 00:14:56.028
CHRIS: Looks cool.

00:14:56.028 --> 00:14:57.863
Good job.

00:14:57.863 --> 00:15:00.532
Alright, now, uh, 40 more?

00:15:00.532 --> 00:15:02.868
JESSI: I was gonna say 75 more.

00:15:02.868 --> 00:15:04.402
Let's get a-millin'.

00:15:04.402 --> 00:15:06.438
♪ ♪

00:15:06.438 --> 00:15:13.045
♪ ♪

00:15:13.045 --> 00:15:16.515
ALEX: Yeah, I really savor
my alone time out here.

00:15:16.515 --> 00:15:19.751
It's, uh, it's not the same
as alone time in the city,

00:15:19.751 --> 00:15:22.654
because there's always noises
and distractions and smell.

00:15:22.654 --> 00:15:25.624
You know what I mean? There's
things you don't even notice.

00:15:25.624 --> 00:15:27.158
You basically, you basically
can't get quiet time

00:15:27.158 --> 00:15:28.294
in the city.

00:15:31.963 --> 00:15:35.434
Today I'm going in there
with the intent to beaver hunt.

00:15:37.168 --> 00:15:41.273
This is the same area
I used to hunt moose at.

00:15:41.273 --> 00:15:43.775
There was always a couple
beavers in it, swimming around.

00:15:43.775 --> 00:15:46.678
And then in the middle,
they have their dam.

00:15:46.678 --> 00:15:48.647
I'm looking for signs of beaver.

00:15:48.647 --> 00:15:50.548
That would be either
tracks in the snow

00:15:50.548 --> 00:15:53.484
or recently chewed-down trees.

00:15:53.484 --> 00:15:54.453
That would indicate that beavers

00:15:54.453 --> 00:15:57.355
are still operating
in this area.

00:15:57.355 --> 00:16:00.559
This is the sign
of a wild beaver.

00:16:00.559 --> 00:16:02.694
Beavers chew down trees.

00:16:02.694 --> 00:16:04.563
That's how they build
their dams.

00:16:07.132 --> 00:16:09.634
The beaver dam's
just right here.

00:16:09.634 --> 00:16:11.003
I'm just gonna
take a look around,

00:16:11.003 --> 00:16:13.305
see if I see tracks,
signs of activity.

00:16:13.305 --> 00:16:17.342
♪ ♪

00:16:17.342 --> 00:16:23.581
♪ ♪

00:16:23.581 --> 00:16:27.619
Kind of doesn't seem like
there's any beavers here.

00:16:27.619 --> 00:16:31.523
I don't see any tracks around.
They may have gone.

00:16:31.523 --> 00:16:32.925
It's getting
pretty dark, though,

00:16:32.925 --> 00:16:36.861
so it might be good
to head home soon.

00:16:36.861 --> 00:16:41.667
♪ ♪

00:16:41.667 --> 00:16:45.237
BRITTANY: Some of the benefits
of living this lifestyle

00:16:45.237 --> 00:16:48.640
is you really value
what you have.

00:16:48.640 --> 00:16:54.880
♪ ♪

00:16:54.880 --> 00:16:59.451
KALEB: It's about
a six-hour, one-way trip

00:16:59.451 --> 00:17:00.719
without the trailer.

00:17:00.719 --> 00:17:02.153
And with the trailer
on this trip,

00:17:02.153 --> 00:17:03.955
it'll be about eight hours.

00:17:03.955 --> 00:17:06.558
It's quite the ordeal.

00:17:06.558 --> 00:17:09.928
And all of that
on icy, nasty roads.

00:17:09.928 --> 00:17:14.432
♪ ♪

00:17:14.432 --> 00:17:17.302
BRITTANY: You have to be
very sure of yourself

00:17:17.302 --> 00:17:19.837
and your vehicle to drive
the road in winter.

00:17:19.837 --> 00:17:24.510
The first thing we have to do
is drive 60 miles of dirt road.

00:17:24.510 --> 00:17:27.279
The McCarthy dirt road is
not for the faint of heart.

00:17:27.279 --> 00:17:30.515
We never know what's
around the next corner.

00:17:30.515 --> 00:17:33.652
KALEB: We're making first tracks
down the road right now

00:17:33.652 --> 00:17:35.954
since it snowed last.

00:17:35.954 --> 00:17:37.655
BRITTANY: Kaleb knows
the McCarthy Road very well,

00:17:37.655 --> 00:17:40.793
but in winter months,
things change so drastically.

00:17:46.197 --> 00:17:49.534
♪ ♪

00:17:49.534 --> 00:17:51.169
Where's the road glaciers?

00:17:51.169 --> 00:17:53.772
KALEB: They're for the next
30 miles or so.

00:17:53.772 --> 00:17:54.706
BRITTANY: Okay.

00:17:54.706 --> 00:17:56.842
KALEB: It is very much
like running a gauntlet

00:17:56.842 --> 00:17:58.576
to get out of here
and into town.

00:17:58.576 --> 00:18:01.980
You really have to want it.

00:18:01.980 --> 00:18:03.481
There's road glaciers

00:18:03.481 --> 00:18:05.918
where water flows across
the road and freezes.

00:18:05.918 --> 00:18:08.186
Some of them can get
10, 12 feet thick.

00:18:08.186 --> 00:18:10.689
That gets downright scary.

00:18:10.689 --> 00:18:13.491
There's one in particular
that's bigger than all the rest,

00:18:13.491 --> 00:18:15.427
and we will be stopping
and checking it

00:18:15.427 --> 00:18:18.196
before we cross it
to make sure it's not too bumpy.

00:18:18.196 --> 00:18:20.332
And hopefully this little bit
of snow that's on the road

00:18:20.332 --> 00:18:23.134
doesn't mask it
where we can't see it.

00:18:23.134 --> 00:18:26.037
That would be very dangerous.

00:18:26.037 --> 00:18:28.974
BRITTANY: Oh.
The joys of this road.

00:18:31.409 --> 00:18:33.177
KALEB: I'm kind of wondering
if we won't even see anybody

00:18:33.177 --> 00:18:34.313
on the road here.

00:18:34.313 --> 00:18:35.914
So if we get into trouble
in any way,

00:18:35.914 --> 00:18:38.183
we're completely on our own.

00:18:38.183 --> 00:18:42.220
Cell phone service along
the road is hit or miss.

00:18:42.220 --> 00:18:45.023
You have to be dedicated
to travel that road.

00:18:45.023 --> 00:18:46.257
You have to know
how get yourself

00:18:46.257 --> 00:18:48.593
out of situations without help.

00:18:48.593 --> 00:18:51.330
It is quite common to travel
the entire length

00:18:51.330 --> 00:18:54.966
of the McCarthy Road
without seeing a single person.

00:18:54.966 --> 00:18:57.001
♪ ♪

00:18:57.001 --> 00:19:03.508
♪ ♪

00:19:03.508 --> 00:19:06.745
BRITTANY: Okay. Whoa. Whoa.

00:19:06.745 --> 00:19:09.148
KALEB: Well, here we go.
Here's our road glacier.

00:19:13.318 --> 00:19:17.054
KALEB: Well, here we go.
Here's our road glacier.

00:19:17.054 --> 00:19:19.491
Thank god for trailer brakes.

00:19:19.491 --> 00:19:20.959
Ah.

00:19:20.959 --> 00:19:26.932
♪ ♪

00:19:26.932 --> 00:19:29.233
Alright, so I,
when I check these out,

00:19:29.233 --> 00:19:30.868
I walk up on the high side here

00:19:30.868 --> 00:19:34.873
and just kind of feel
how slippery it is.

00:19:34.873 --> 00:19:35.941
And I look for this,

00:19:35.941 --> 00:19:39.077
this is some slush here,
which gives good traction.

00:19:39.077 --> 00:19:42.080
So if I've got slush,
we're pretty much good to go.

00:19:42.080 --> 00:19:43.815
BRITTANY:
Think we'll make it across?

00:19:43.815 --> 00:19:45.550
KALEB: Let's give her a go.

00:19:45.550 --> 00:19:52.524
♪ ♪

00:19:52.524 --> 00:19:59.330
♪ ♪

00:19:59.330 --> 00:20:06.337
♪ ♪

00:20:06.337 --> 00:20:07.439
Oh, that one was good.

00:20:07.439 --> 00:20:09.340
BRITTANY: Do you think those
are gonna get worse?

00:20:09.340 --> 00:20:11.275
KALEB: Oh, they're gonna
get much worse.

00:20:11.275 --> 00:20:13.745
We're at the beginning
of the winter right now.

00:20:13.745 --> 00:20:16.281
They've only just
started to form.

00:20:19.517 --> 00:20:21.886
Right here is
the Kuskulana Bridge.

00:20:21.886 --> 00:20:25.757
It's 256 feet up above
the river bottom.

00:20:25.757 --> 00:20:30.795
♪ ♪

00:20:30.795 --> 00:20:35.834
♪ ♪

00:20:35.834 --> 00:20:42.574
♪ ♪

00:20:42.574 --> 00:20:45.143
We've made it through the
toughest part of the road here.

00:20:45.143 --> 00:20:48.279
All the road glaciers
and potholes are behind us,

00:20:48.279 --> 00:20:52.550
so now it's pretty much just
smooth sailing on into the city.

00:20:52.550 --> 00:20:55.653
I'm hoping to spend as little
amount of time in the city

00:20:55.653 --> 00:20:57.122
as possible tomorrow,

00:20:57.122 --> 00:21:00.825
so we need to be quick and
efficient in all our stops.

00:21:00.825 --> 00:21:03.594
I really dislike
going into the city.

00:21:03.594 --> 00:21:05.597
It's always stressful for me.

00:21:05.597 --> 00:21:08.867
And I'm always just wanting
to get in and get out.

00:21:10.802 --> 00:21:14.373
BRITTANY:
We are in the city! Woo-hoo!

00:21:14.373 --> 00:21:16.708
KALEB: Oh, boy.

00:21:16.708 --> 00:21:21.546
♪ ♪

00:21:21.546 --> 00:21:23.348
CHRIS: We're always trying
to look ahead as far as we can,

00:21:23.348 --> 00:21:26.317
and long-term goals out here
are great to have,

00:21:26.317 --> 00:21:29.421
but sometimes you just have to
focus on what's in front of you.

00:21:30.922 --> 00:21:34.459
(chainsaw roaring)

00:21:37.262 --> 00:21:39.898
Hey, Jess, how many boards
do we have?

00:21:39.898 --> 00:21:43.134
JESSI: 22, 23, 24, 25.

00:21:43.134 --> 00:21:44.603
CHRIS:
We're right on the money, 25.

00:21:44.603 --> 00:21:45.570
JESSI: 25.

00:21:45.570 --> 00:21:46.404
CHRIS: We're gonna get
a few more out of this

00:21:46.404 --> 00:21:48.072
and we'll be done.

00:21:48.072 --> 00:21:49.541
JESSI: Yeah!

00:21:51.443 --> 00:21:53.444
CHRIS: My dad built this cabin
and left it to me.

00:21:53.444 --> 00:21:54.845
And I haven't had a chance
to come out here

00:21:54.845 --> 00:21:56.280
and really build any structures.

00:21:56.280 --> 00:21:57.449
Once I get this structure done,

00:21:57.449 --> 00:21:59.484
I'm gonna feel like I have
accomplished something out here,

00:21:59.484 --> 00:22:01.085
and I've adding something
to the homestead.

00:22:01.085 --> 00:22:04.088
That's gonna feel good.

00:22:04.088 --> 00:22:05.223
JESSI: Yes!

00:22:05.223 --> 00:22:07.158
Last cut, love.

00:22:07.158 --> 00:22:08.360
Last one!

00:22:08.360 --> 00:22:09.661
Yes!

00:22:12.364 --> 00:22:14.399
Ready to build shed!

00:22:18.303 --> 00:22:19.704
Okay.

00:22:19.704 --> 00:22:21.906
CHRIS: So what we need is
about 12-foot-long birch poles

00:22:21.906 --> 00:22:23.708
that are about
four inches in diameter.

00:22:23.708 --> 00:22:24.876
We're gonna put those
at the top,

00:22:24.876 --> 00:22:26.678
and that's gonna be
the main roof supports.

00:22:26.678 --> 00:22:29.480
Followed by a couple
smaller beams going down

00:22:29.480 --> 00:22:32.317
to support the planks
that we have made.

00:22:32.317 --> 00:22:33.718
Let's see if we can get
about 10 of them.

00:22:33.718 --> 00:22:35.286
JESSI: Okay.

00:22:35.286 --> 00:22:36.654
CHRIS: Alright.
JESSI: I will haul them back.

00:22:36.654 --> 00:22:37.621
CHRIS: It's getting cold out.

00:22:37.621 --> 00:22:39.925
JESSI: I know. Winter's coming.

00:22:42.693 --> 00:22:46.331
(chainsaw roaring)

00:22:46.331 --> 00:22:51.969
♪ ♪

00:22:51.969 --> 00:22:53.170
CHRIS: When you're
out here building

00:22:53.170 --> 00:22:55.940
without a wood supply store,
and you don't have everything,

00:22:55.940 --> 00:22:59.744
you just have to build
with what nature gives you.

00:22:59.744 --> 00:23:01.246
(grunts)

00:23:01.246 --> 00:23:02.680
Watch out!

00:23:02.680 --> 00:23:08.219
♪ ♪

00:23:08.219 --> 00:23:10.888
The real reason we're using
the trees that are already here

00:23:10.888 --> 00:23:13.224
is that they make really good
permanent structures.

00:23:13.224 --> 00:23:14.625
They're not gonna heave
with the frost.

00:23:14.625 --> 00:23:16.161
They've been here a long time.

00:23:16.161 --> 00:23:18.529
I'm not worried about them
caving under the snow load.

00:23:18.529 --> 00:23:19.864
This is gonna add
a lot of structure

00:23:19.864 --> 00:23:22.800
having these extra trees.

00:23:22.800 --> 00:23:24.502
JESSI: I've never built anything

00:23:24.502 --> 00:23:29.340
that I didn't get every single
part from a hardware store.

00:23:29.340 --> 00:23:31.209
And we made that
pumper out here.

00:23:31.209 --> 00:23:33.578
And, you know, most of
those nails were burned out

00:23:33.578 --> 00:23:37.048
from who knows when.

00:23:37.048 --> 00:23:38.917
JESSI: There we go.
CHRIS: Nice!

00:23:38.917 --> 00:23:39.850
CHRIS: Our next step here

00:23:39.850 --> 00:23:41.787
is we're gonna start
putting the planks up.

00:23:43.955 --> 00:23:45.823
First one there, huh?

00:23:45.823 --> 00:23:48.192
So, Jess, take a look at this.

00:23:48.192 --> 00:23:50.228
I'm gonna put you in charge
of drilling holes,

00:23:50.228 --> 00:23:53.331
and I'm gonna hammer nails.

00:23:53.331 --> 00:23:55.900
(drilling)

00:23:55.900 --> 00:23:57.735
Alright.

00:23:57.735 --> 00:23:58.569
You know, I didn't really know

00:23:58.569 --> 00:23:59.671
what I was building
when I started.

00:23:59.671 --> 00:24:02.507
I knew the general idea,
I wanted a triangle shed.

00:24:02.507 --> 00:24:06.511
As I go along, I just take it as
it is and build with what I got.

00:24:06.511 --> 00:24:07.312
Nice!

00:24:07.312 --> 00:24:09.180
JESSI: Yeah, shed! Yes!

00:24:09.180 --> 00:24:11.683
CHRIS: Coming along.

00:24:11.683 --> 00:24:15.186
You know, my dad handmade
this cabin with no power tools.

00:24:15.186 --> 00:24:17.756
Just a hand whip and a hammer.

00:24:19.290 --> 00:24:21.226
He didn't have the tools
to make a perfect house,

00:24:21.226 --> 00:24:23.761
but that's okay;
he built a home.

00:24:23.761 --> 00:24:25.529
You're not gonna
always do it right;

00:24:25.529 --> 00:24:27.065
just keep trying.

00:24:27.065 --> 00:24:29.267
He always told me that.

00:24:29.267 --> 00:24:32.303
My dad moved out to the bush
when he was about 19.

00:24:32.303 --> 00:24:34.972
And here I am in my 30s
moving out to the bush.

00:24:34.972 --> 00:24:37.341
That's when he came
out of the bush,

00:24:37.341 --> 00:24:39.044
which kind of makes me feel
like I'm just picking up

00:24:39.044 --> 00:24:41.012
where Dad left off.

00:24:42.480 --> 00:24:44.215
Getting out there,
building on the house,

00:24:44.215 --> 00:24:46.952
making things work.

00:24:46.952 --> 00:24:49.621
I feel like I'm following
in my dad's footsteps.

00:24:51.656 --> 00:24:53.691
♪ ♪

00:24:53.691 --> 00:24:59.230
♪ ♪

00:24:59.230 --> 00:25:01.499
MICHAEL: In the long run,
you could pay for convenience,

00:25:01.499 --> 00:25:05.169
but sometimes you got to take
things into your own hands

00:25:05.169 --> 00:25:06.872
and build it right for you.

00:25:08.573 --> 00:25:12.610
(dogs barking)

00:25:12.610 --> 00:25:15.013
You better eat your meal.

00:25:15.013 --> 00:25:17.682
You got to be
my crash test dummy.

00:25:17.682 --> 00:25:19.150
Go ahead.

00:25:19.150 --> 00:25:20.951
You ready for a new sled, Snoop?

00:25:20.951 --> 00:25:22.453
You ready to break a new sled?

00:25:22.453 --> 00:25:24.889
You're not gonna break this one.

00:25:24.889 --> 00:25:28.593
You're not gonna break
this sled, big boy.

00:25:28.593 --> 00:25:30.928
 NARRATOR: In order to hunt
 and gather resources,

00:25:30.928 --> 00:25:33.064
 Michael Manzo has
 completed construction

00:25:33.064 --> 00:25:35.799
 on a custom freight sled.

00:25:35.799 --> 00:25:37.702
 But in order to test his work,

00:25:37.702 --> 00:25:42.040
 he'll need to run it across
 the harsh Alaskan terrain.

00:25:42.040 --> 00:25:44.943
MICHAEL: Last night, I got the
brunt of this sled build done

00:25:44.943 --> 00:25:48.580
well into the evening,
and now it's the test.

00:25:50.415 --> 00:25:52.316
I wanna see if it
can actually hold up

00:25:52.316 --> 00:25:56.988
to three of my most brutal dogs
in the lot.

00:25:56.988 --> 00:25:59.924
Prime, Snoop, and Cyran.

00:25:59.924 --> 00:26:03.361
My biggest, most powerful dogs
in the lot.

00:26:05.162 --> 00:26:07.098
They're terrible dogs
to get out of the yard,

00:26:07.098 --> 00:26:10.067
because they destroy stuff.

00:26:10.067 --> 00:26:12.436
I don't approve of the design.

00:26:12.436 --> 00:26:14.071
Snoop approves of the design.

00:26:14.071 --> 00:26:15.573
(barks)

00:26:15.573 --> 00:26:17.442
Wanna go for a run?

00:26:17.442 --> 00:26:24.316
(barking)

00:26:24.316 --> 00:26:25.850
Dogs are crazy.

00:26:25.850 --> 00:26:31.489
(barking)

00:26:31.489 --> 00:26:33.158
It moves so easy.

00:26:33.158 --> 00:26:34.592
Look at that.

00:26:34.592 --> 00:26:36.160
Look at this.

00:26:36.160 --> 00:26:37.629
Moves on a pinkie.

00:26:37.629 --> 00:26:40.932
(barking)

00:26:40.932 --> 00:26:42.800
That thing is slick.

00:26:42.800 --> 00:26:45.570
There's a lot of uncertainty
with running this sled.

00:26:45.570 --> 00:26:48.139
It's an entirely new design.

00:26:48.139 --> 00:26:52.777
It is a very long sled
in a tight, wooded trail.

00:26:52.777 --> 00:26:55.580
It's 11 foot long
from the front of the bow

00:26:55.580 --> 00:26:58.883
to the back of the runner,

00:26:58.883 --> 00:27:02.954
and that's a little
nerve-racking.

00:27:02.954 --> 00:27:07.525
Basket sleds of that length are
synonymous for not steering.

00:27:07.525 --> 00:27:10.361
I designed everything
as strongly as I could

00:27:10.361 --> 00:27:12.763
and as flexible as I could,

00:27:12.763 --> 00:27:15.800
but I'll give it
a good test and see.

00:27:18.269 --> 00:27:22.874
Alright. Time to harness
these crazy dogs up.

00:27:22.874 --> 00:27:24.409
(barking)

00:27:24.409 --> 00:27:26.211
You ready to go for a run,
buddy?

00:27:26.211 --> 00:27:29.414
They shred stuff.
They destroy sleds.

00:27:29.414 --> 00:27:31.716
They don't care that
that sled is long.

00:27:31.716 --> 00:27:33.518
They're just gonna go.

00:27:35.453 --> 00:27:36.487
Double-check everything.

00:27:36.487 --> 00:27:38.956
Make sure I screwed
these carabiners down,

00:27:38.956 --> 00:27:42.293
'cause this is a lot of dog.

00:27:42.293 --> 00:27:45.163
These dogs are brutal.

00:27:45.163 --> 00:27:46.130
(barking)

00:27:46.130 --> 00:27:47.265
Alright.

00:27:47.265 --> 00:27:50.334
I got to get these guys on
the trail, they're going crazy.

00:27:50.334 --> 00:27:52.303
Go get 'em! Hyah! Hyah! Hyah!

00:27:57.041 --> 00:27:59.276
(barking)

00:27:59.276 --> 00:28:00.612
MICHAEL: I got to get
these guys on the trail,

00:28:00.612 --> 00:28:02.579
they're going crazy.

00:28:02.579 --> 00:28:05.016
Go get 'em! Hyah! Hyah! Hyah!

00:28:05.016 --> 00:28:09.320
(barking)

00:28:11.088 --> 00:28:13.224
Go get 'em! Hyah! Hyah! Haw!

00:28:13.224 --> 00:28:15.760
(barking)

00:28:15.760 --> 00:28:19.263
Whoa, whoa. Hey, hey, hey!
Get up there! Come on!

00:28:19.263 --> 00:28:22.132
(barking)

00:28:22.132 --> 00:28:25.169
Go get 'em! Hyah! Hyah! Hyah!

00:28:25.169 --> 00:28:28.073
Come on. Hop up. Hop up. Hyah!

00:28:32.310 --> 00:28:35.647
♪ ♪

00:28:35.647 --> 00:28:38.616
This is a lot.

00:28:38.616 --> 00:28:42.520
Just starting out with a fresh
sled on a tight, wooded trail.

00:28:42.520 --> 00:28:44.822
Usually when
you test a sled out,

00:28:44.822 --> 00:28:47.224
you'd wanna test it
in the open country,

00:28:47.224 --> 00:28:49.393
or on a lake,
or out on the sea ice

00:28:49.393 --> 00:28:51.729
where you're not gonna
crash into stuff.

00:28:51.729 --> 00:28:54.031
I'm kind of taking it
to another level.

00:28:54.031 --> 00:28:56.067
♪ ♪

00:28:56.067 --> 00:29:01.305
♪ ♪

00:29:01.305 --> 00:29:03.607
Wow, that steered good!

00:29:03.607 --> 00:29:04.843
Dang!

00:29:04.843 --> 00:29:07.879
See that tight corner?
It's taking corners, man.

00:29:07.879 --> 00:29:10.582
I can lean into it.

00:29:10.582 --> 00:29:13.917
I get a lot of motivation
from this dog team of mine.

00:29:13.917 --> 00:29:16.020
They're a really rowdy bunch.

00:29:16.020 --> 00:29:19.089
And we're doing new things,
we're going new places,

00:29:19.089 --> 00:29:21.593
and we're getting better
with each year.

00:29:23.260 --> 00:29:26.264
I've broken a half dozen
different sleds

00:29:26.264 --> 00:29:29.266
over the past five years,

00:29:29.266 --> 00:29:33.104
but this one here,
it's gonna be a tough sled.

00:29:33.104 --> 00:29:36.707
And I can haul way more than
I could ever imagine.

00:29:36.707 --> 00:29:39.143
The amount of capability
that I'm gaining

00:29:39.143 --> 00:29:41.379
with this sled is insane.

00:29:41.379 --> 00:29:43.615
Woo!

00:29:43.615 --> 00:29:46.517
It's a fun run!

00:29:46.517 --> 00:29:53.324
♪ ♪

00:29:53.324 --> 00:29:55.059
ALEX: At the end of the day,
I try to think,

00:29:55.059 --> 00:30:00.431
okay, how much time do I have
left before I call this quits?

00:30:00.431 --> 00:30:04.535
Before it's going past the point
of diminishing returns?

00:30:04.535 --> 00:30:11.475
♪ ♪

00:30:11.475 --> 00:30:14.011
Last night, it rained
and got really warm

00:30:14.011 --> 00:30:17.214
and everything melted.

00:30:17.214 --> 00:30:21.286
This will make finding
tracks of animals,

00:30:21.286 --> 00:30:24.288
specifically beavers down there,
probably impossible.

00:30:24.288 --> 00:30:26.890
It'll probably be all melted.

00:30:26.890 --> 00:30:28.326
But I think I'll still
go down there

00:30:28.326 --> 00:30:31.462
and I'll look for any
recently chewed trees.

00:30:31.462 --> 00:30:36.100
Yeah, I haven't had a good
protein dinner in a while.

00:30:36.100 --> 00:30:39.237
It would be nice, but,
you know, there's lean times

00:30:39.237 --> 00:30:42.172
and then there's,
there's non-lean times.

00:30:42.172 --> 00:30:44.309
Right now is a lean time, so.

00:30:45.876 --> 00:30:47.278
I'm gonna head back out,

00:30:47.278 --> 00:30:49.480
looking to see if
I missed anything yesterday,

00:30:49.480 --> 00:30:51.782
see if there's
any sign of beaver.

00:30:51.782 --> 00:30:54.018
I don't have my fingers crossed
on this, but you never know.

00:30:54.018 --> 00:30:56.421
It's always good to
double-check and make sure.

00:30:59.490 --> 00:31:04.928
I never paid attention to the
distances out here or the time.

00:31:04.928 --> 00:31:08.466
I kind of measure things in
terms of how tired it makes me

00:31:08.466 --> 00:31:11.202
or how much effort it takes.

00:31:11.202 --> 00:31:13.270
So it can be hard to explain
to other people.

00:31:13.270 --> 00:31:14.071
A lot of times people ask me,

00:31:14.071 --> 00:31:15.172
"How far is it
from here to here?"

00:31:15.172 --> 00:31:16.907
And I say, "Uh, I don't know,
an hour maybe."

00:31:16.907 --> 00:31:18.843
But I honestly have no idea.

00:31:18.843 --> 00:31:21.412
That's just kind of
what it feels like to me.

00:31:21.412 --> 00:31:24.315
But I live out here alone,
so it works for me.

00:31:24.315 --> 00:31:30.021
♪ ♪

00:31:30.021 --> 00:31:34.091
Well, back here
at the beaver dam.

00:31:34.091 --> 00:31:36.393
I think the owners are gone.

00:31:36.393 --> 00:31:38.429
I took a look
around the perimeter,

00:31:38.429 --> 00:31:41.899
tried to see if I could see any,
like, recently chewed trees.

00:31:41.899 --> 00:31:44.002
They all look pretty old.

00:31:44.002 --> 00:31:47.572
I think these beavers probably
left a year or more ago.

00:31:47.572 --> 00:31:49.440
So beaver's off the menu.

00:31:49.440 --> 00:31:56.380
♪ ♪

00:31:56.380 --> 00:31:57.314
It's a little disappointing.

00:31:57.314 --> 00:31:59.583
Beaver would have been
a great, great meal.

00:31:59.583 --> 00:32:01.185
But it is what it is.

00:32:01.185 --> 00:32:03.688
I always try to be open
to learning from failure

00:32:03.688 --> 00:32:07.992
and not let failure
disappoint me.

00:32:07.992 --> 00:32:10.828
(clucking)

00:32:10.828 --> 00:32:12.263
(fluttering)

00:32:15.299 --> 00:32:19.370
I, uh, I heard grouse wings.

00:32:19.370 --> 00:32:24.475
I think he just moved from one
tree over here, over to there.

00:32:24.475 --> 00:32:26.343
Grouse, they, they make
a distinctive noise.

00:32:26.343 --> 00:32:27.678
As soon as I hear the sound,

00:32:27.678 --> 00:32:30.047
I get my eyes up
and just watch and listen.

00:32:30.047 --> 00:32:31.482
If you can spot where they are,

00:32:31.482 --> 00:32:32.616
usually you can see
where they land

00:32:32.616 --> 00:32:33.884
'cause they don't land too far.

00:32:33.884 --> 00:32:37.388
They try to rely on their
camouflage to avoid detection.

00:32:39.691 --> 00:32:43.561
He's just right ahead of me
in that tree there.

00:32:43.561 --> 00:32:49.600
♪ ♪

00:32:49.600 --> 00:32:53.871
♪ ♪

00:32:53.871 --> 00:32:54.572
(gunshot)

00:32:59.476 --> 00:33:03.847
♪ ♪

00:33:03.847 --> 00:33:06.250
♪ ♪

00:33:06.250 --> 00:33:07.251
(gunshot)

00:33:07.251 --> 00:33:10.388
♪ ♪

00:33:14.391 --> 00:33:18.996
♪ ♪

00:33:18.996 --> 00:33:25.836
♪ ♪

00:33:25.836 --> 00:33:27.204
ALEX: Yeah.

00:33:27.204 --> 00:33:33.677
♪ ♪

00:33:33.677 --> 00:33:35.880
Looks like we got him
through the heart.

00:33:35.880 --> 00:33:38.382
It's a decent-sized one.

00:33:38.382 --> 00:33:43.287
So, uh, there's a nice dinner.

00:33:43.287 --> 00:33:44.956
It's pretty awesome.

00:33:46.890 --> 00:33:48.292
It's still a good place to hunt.

00:33:48.292 --> 00:33:49.760
Even if beavers
are off the table,

00:33:49.760 --> 00:33:53.497
it's always good to hunt
different areas.

00:33:53.497 --> 00:33:54.866
We'll go ahead and
field dress it here,

00:33:54.866 --> 00:33:56.767
because the weather
is pretty is warm,

00:33:56.767 --> 00:34:00.137
I don't want the meat
to get spoiled.

00:34:00.137 --> 00:34:01.339
So by taking out the guts

00:34:01.339 --> 00:34:02.973
and just removing
just the meat you want,

00:34:02.973 --> 00:34:04.942
taking off all the feathers,

00:34:04.942 --> 00:34:06.944
it'll help the meat
cool down quicker.

00:34:09.113 --> 00:34:10.314
Good enough.

00:34:10.314 --> 00:34:12.416
I'll just rip out the breast
and the wings.

00:34:12.416 --> 00:34:14.419
The rest is just gut piles.

00:34:17.654 --> 00:34:19.289
The reason I always take
a backpack full

00:34:19.289 --> 00:34:21.092
of what you might call possibles

00:34:21.092 --> 00:34:23.194
is because there's
always a possibility

00:34:23.194 --> 00:34:25.462
you come across an opportunity
you didn't plan for.

00:34:25.462 --> 00:34:28.165
In this case,
I was going to find beavers.

00:34:28.165 --> 00:34:31.902
There's no beavers,
so, tough luck there.

00:34:31.902 --> 00:34:34.572
But I managed to get
a grouse today.

00:34:34.572 --> 00:34:36.507
I can return home happy.

00:34:36.507 --> 00:34:38.442
Have a nice dinner tonight.

00:34:38.442 --> 00:34:40.945
That's always very encouraging.

00:34:42.446 --> 00:34:47.051
♪ ♪

00:34:47.051 --> 00:34:50.387
KALEB: While this life
may not be more complicated

00:34:50.387 --> 00:34:51.522
than life in the city,

00:34:51.522 --> 00:34:56.560
it does require its own
unique set of challenges,

00:34:56.560 --> 00:34:58.129
which are usually overcome

00:34:58.129 --> 00:35:00.765
with proper planning
and due diligence.

00:35:02.733 --> 00:35:06.737
♪ ♪

00:35:06.737 --> 00:35:09.072
BRITTANY: So what are we doing
first this morning?

00:35:09.072 --> 00:35:11.441
KALEB: So we're dropping the
vehicle off at the scrapyard,

00:35:11.441 --> 00:35:14.411
and then we're gonna be headed
to the hardware store,

00:35:14.411 --> 00:35:16.614
pick up a load
of building materials.

00:35:20.017 --> 00:35:22.320
Definitely ready to have it
off of my trailer

00:35:22.320 --> 00:35:25.189
and be done with it.

00:35:25.189 --> 00:35:27.291
Alright, we're here.

00:35:27.291 --> 00:35:28.459
BRITTANY: Sweet.

00:35:33.965 --> 00:35:36.634
KALEB: It feels great getting
this vehicle dropped off

00:35:36.634 --> 00:35:37.801
at the junkyard.

00:35:37.801 --> 00:35:42.573
It's yet another eyesore
gone from my valley.

00:35:46.878 --> 00:35:49.679
So far the weather has been
holding off for us.

00:35:49.679 --> 00:35:51.315
However, there's a weather front

00:35:51.315 --> 00:35:53.451
moving into
the Copper River Valley.

00:35:53.451 --> 00:35:56.454
So we're gonna try and beat
that weather system home.

00:35:56.454 --> 00:35:58.289
Alright, we're here.

00:35:59.824 --> 00:36:02.026
BRITTANY: Can we get
a nap after this?

00:36:02.026 --> 00:36:04.428
KALEB: Um, no.

00:36:04.428 --> 00:36:07.131
The lumber supplier
is 300 miles away.

00:36:07.131 --> 00:36:08.199
You have to pay attention

00:36:08.199 --> 00:36:12.235
and make sure your preparation
is done in advance.

00:36:12.235 --> 00:36:13.204
KALEB: Thank you much.
MAN: Alrighty.

00:36:13.204 --> 00:36:14.138
KALEB: Somebody will be out
with a forklift?

00:36:14.138 --> 00:36:16.407
MAN: Yup.
KALEB: Sweet.

00:36:18.943 --> 00:36:21.011
KALEB: I'm picking up
some two-bys, some plywood,

00:36:21.011 --> 00:36:24.081
and some other odds and ends
of building materials.

00:36:24.081 --> 00:36:27.418
We're gonna put about
7,500 pounds on the trailer.

00:36:27.418 --> 00:36:28.519
It'll weigh it down nice.

00:36:28.519 --> 00:36:31.722
Should give it lots
of traction on the road.

00:36:31.722 --> 00:36:35.725
♪ ♪

00:36:35.725 --> 00:36:37.395
Starting to snow a little bit.

00:36:37.395 --> 00:36:38.295
BRITTANY: Yeah.

00:36:38.295 --> 00:36:41.364
KALEB: Hopefully the roads
will be good.

00:36:41.364 --> 00:36:42.366
BRITTANY: How's the weight feel?

00:36:42.366 --> 00:36:44.201
Is it any different
from the truck?

00:36:44.201 --> 00:36:45.502
KALEB: Oh, it's
a little bit heavier,

00:36:45.502 --> 00:36:47.104
but it feels well-distributed,

00:36:47.104 --> 00:36:48.973
and it doesn't bounce
when we go over bumps.

00:36:48.973 --> 00:36:49.974
BRITTANY: Oh, that's nice.

00:36:49.974 --> 00:36:51.609
KALEB: Which feels so much nicer
than the truck.

00:36:51.609 --> 00:36:52.543
BRITTANY: Yeah.

00:36:52.543 --> 00:36:54.378
That'll be really nice
on our way home.

00:36:54.378 --> 00:36:56.113
We have lots of bounces.

00:36:56.113 --> 00:36:57.080
KALEB: Lots of bumps.

00:36:57.080 --> 00:36:57.881
BRITTANY: Yeah. That's what I...

00:36:57.881 --> 00:37:01.252
KALEB: Frost heaves, potholes.

00:37:01.252 --> 00:37:04.021
And that's just
on the paved section.

00:37:04.021 --> 00:37:06.256
That's not even
the McCarthy Road.

00:37:06.256 --> 00:37:12.362
♪ ♪

00:37:12.362 --> 00:37:13.530
Dumb light.

00:37:13.530 --> 00:37:15.198
BRITTANY: Oh, man.

00:37:15.198 --> 00:37:18.502
Aren't you glad
we have none in McCarthy?

00:37:18.502 --> 00:37:20.003
KALEB: I am so glad.

00:37:20.003 --> 00:37:21.771
Brittney really would
like to stay longer,

00:37:21.771 --> 00:37:23.541
but we need to get going home.

00:37:23.541 --> 00:37:25.676
BRITTANY: We don't want to get
caught up in the storm.

00:37:25.676 --> 00:37:28.045
It sounds like it's gonna be
a pretty bad one with rain,

00:37:28.045 --> 00:37:30.614
which is never fun
this time of year.

00:37:30.614 --> 00:37:33.617
KALEB: Slippery highways,
here we come.

00:37:33.617 --> 00:37:36.220
See you later, city.

00:37:36.220 --> 00:37:43.060
♪ ♪

00:37:43.060 --> 00:37:46.664
CHRIS: Every time I make a plan,
I have to change it.

00:37:46.664 --> 00:37:48.332
So I just like to let
the universe guide me

00:37:48.332 --> 00:37:51.301
and just build what I build.

00:37:51.301 --> 00:37:54.905
♪ ♪

00:37:54.905 --> 00:37:58.843
(hammering)

00:37:58.843 --> 00:37:59.644
Alright, love.

00:37:59.644 --> 00:38:00.444
JESSI: The roof!

00:38:00.444 --> 00:38:01.979
CHRIS: Got a roof!

00:38:01.979 --> 00:38:03.247
(grunts)

00:38:03.247 --> 00:38:05.750
JESSI: Okay. Look at that!

00:38:08.586 --> 00:38:10.154
CHRIS: There it is.

00:38:12.956 --> 00:38:15.625
JESSI: Yes!

00:38:15.625 --> 00:38:16.426
CHRIS: Good work.

00:38:16.426 --> 00:38:18.161
JESSI: Yay, shed!

00:38:18.161 --> 00:38:19.863
Let's get that three-wheeler.

00:38:19.863 --> 00:38:22.132
Show it its home.

00:38:22.132 --> 00:38:23.601
The shed is done.

00:38:23.601 --> 00:38:25.169
I am so stoked.

00:38:25.169 --> 00:38:26.970
Gallons of sawdust.

00:38:26.970 --> 00:38:30.141
My hair and my eyes
will never be the same.

00:38:30.141 --> 00:38:32.176
And we're done.

00:38:32.176 --> 00:38:36.646
That seems pretty
stinking solid!

00:38:36.646 --> 00:38:37.781
CHRIS: I'm not the best
builder in the world,

00:38:37.781 --> 00:38:40.650
but the project
came together pretty well.

00:38:40.650 --> 00:38:43.187
(engine sputters)
Awesome. Uh--agh!

00:38:48.859 --> 00:38:53.363
(engine revving)

00:38:53.363 --> 00:38:54.732
(click)

00:38:57.735 --> 00:39:00.705
(revving)

00:39:03.140 --> 00:39:04.642
JESSI: Yeah!

00:39:07.111 --> 00:39:08.245
I love our shed.

00:39:08.245 --> 00:39:09.713
I love what it looks like.

00:39:09.713 --> 00:39:12.315
I love that it fits
the three-wheeler.

00:39:12.315 --> 00:39:14.318
CHRIS: I feel pretty confident
that I did a good job

00:39:14.318 --> 00:39:17.187
and it's gonna
withstand the bears.

00:39:17.187 --> 00:39:19.589
(grunts)

00:39:19.589 --> 00:39:21.225
JESSI: Which side?

00:39:21.225 --> 00:39:22.960
CHRIS: Your side first.

00:39:27.131 --> 00:39:30.300
JESSI: It's useable,
it's functional, it's beautiful.

00:39:30.300 --> 00:39:32.836
Our stuff is gonna be way
more safe when we're not here.

00:39:32.836 --> 00:39:34.504
Chris and I did good.

00:39:34.504 --> 00:39:41.445
♪ ♪

00:39:41.445 --> 00:39:42.713
BRITTANY: I'm glad your parents
could keep the kids

00:39:42.713 --> 00:39:44.849
another night;
it's about their bedtime.

00:39:44.849 --> 00:39:45.983
KALEB: Uh, yeah.

00:39:45.983 --> 00:39:47.117
BRITTANY: We still have
another what? Hour and a half?

00:39:47.117 --> 00:39:49.019
KALEB: Still another
30 miles to get home.

00:39:49.019 --> 00:39:50.254
BRITTANY: Goodness.

00:39:53.857 --> 00:39:54.892
KALEB: So I think
we're gonna put chains

00:39:54.892 --> 00:39:56.293
on the two front tires.

00:39:56.293 --> 00:39:59.263
Just for safety,
for the rest of the trips.

00:39:59.263 --> 00:40:00.498
Can you run the spotlight?

00:40:00.498 --> 00:40:02.466
BRITTANY: Yeah.

00:40:02.466 --> 00:40:06.803
KALEB: It's a difficult enough
road to travel during the day.

00:40:06.803 --> 00:40:08.405
And throwing darkness
into the mix

00:40:08.405 --> 00:40:11.942
just makes it that much harder.

00:40:11.942 --> 00:40:14.145
I'm ready to get
out of this rain.

00:40:16.746 --> 00:40:19.716
Alright. That's nice and tight.

00:40:19.716 --> 00:40:21.418
BRITTANY: Alright, well,
let's get out of the wind.

00:40:21.418 --> 00:40:23.353
I'm frozen.

00:40:23.353 --> 00:40:30.193
♪ ♪

00:40:30.193 --> 00:40:33.097
KALEB: We're gonna be super
careful; it's 50 degrees out.

00:40:33.097 --> 00:40:36.833
There's slick ice on the road,
and it's raining and blowing.

00:40:36.833 --> 00:40:39.302
So we're getting hit
from all sides here.

00:40:39.302 --> 00:40:42.239
♪ ♪

00:40:45.108 --> 00:40:51.748
♪ ♪

00:40:51.748 --> 00:40:56.220
ALEX: It's really nice to have
this nature-provided dinner.

00:40:56.220 --> 00:40:59.590
Be able to cook it outside
in this nice weather.

00:40:59.590 --> 00:41:05.195
This isn't some disgusting,
factory farmed, sickly bird

00:41:05.195 --> 00:41:07.564
that had a terrible life.

00:41:07.564 --> 00:41:08.832
This was a beautiful,

00:41:08.832 --> 00:41:10.434
magnificent creature
of the woods.

00:41:10.434 --> 00:41:13.103
It was strong. It was powerful.
It was healthy.

00:41:15.505 --> 00:41:18.275
I was there with it
in its final moments.

00:41:18.275 --> 00:41:21.011
(sizzling)

00:41:23.113 --> 00:41:26.449
I really enjoy quiet solitude
here at the cabin.

00:41:26.449 --> 00:41:27.651
And the weather's nice and warm.

00:41:27.651 --> 00:41:31.388
Just sit by the stove
and watch the trees grow,

00:41:31.388 --> 00:41:33.156
listen to the wind.

00:41:33.156 --> 00:41:36.193
♪ ♪

00:41:36.193 --> 00:41:42.599
♪ ♪

00:41:42.599 --> 00:41:45.769
 ♪ Oh, my lord, take this soul ♪

00:41:45.769 --> 00:41:48.905
 ♪ Lay me at the bottom
 of the river ♪

00:41:48.905 --> 00:41:52.142
 ♪ The devil has come
 to carry me home ♪

00:41:52.142 --> 00:41:54.611
 ♪ Lay me at the bottom ♪

00:41:54.611 --> 00:41:57.847
 ♪ The bottom of the river ♪

00:41:57.847 --> 00:41:59.483
MICHAEL: I felt dead
for a long time

00:41:59.483 --> 00:42:03.219
after my three
one-year tours in Iraq,

00:42:03.219 --> 00:42:06.090
and I was very frustrated
with the world.

00:42:08.792 --> 00:42:11.795
And it took many years
to actually find things

00:42:11.795 --> 00:42:14.498
that are passionate and fun.

00:42:14.498 --> 00:42:16.466
I really enjoy the journey

00:42:16.466 --> 00:42:18.969
of traversing the landscape
by dog team.

00:42:18.969 --> 00:42:23.907
It's just, it's a great
breath of fresh air.

00:42:23.907 --> 00:42:25.075
Nice catch.

00:42:25.075 --> 00:42:28.745
♪ ♪

00:42:28.745 --> 00:42:30.013
CHRIS: When my dad moved out
to the Cosna,

00:42:30.013 --> 00:42:32.282
it was during the Homestead Act.

00:42:32.282 --> 00:42:33.484
There was already
a bunch of homesteaders

00:42:33.484 --> 00:42:34.851
up there settled in.

00:42:34.851 --> 00:42:36.720
There was a whole
slew of people.

00:42:36.720 --> 00:42:37.521
JESSI: Oh, man.

00:42:37.521 --> 00:42:38.621
CHRIS: Heavy.

00:42:38.621 --> 00:42:40.123
He had a little neighborhood.

00:42:40.123 --> 00:42:42.559
He had friends to help him
lift logs onto his cabin.

00:42:42.559 --> 00:42:43.694
Those days have passed.

00:42:43.694 --> 00:42:45.896
We're pretty much the only
homesteaders out there anymore,

00:42:45.896 --> 00:42:48.331
so we got to turn
to each other for help.

00:42:48.331 --> 00:42:50.934
And without my wife,
it wouldn't be possible.

00:42:50.934 --> 00:42:54.104
We're a team in this.

00:42:54.104 --> 00:42:56.139
♪ ♪

00:42:56.139 --> 00:43:01.578
♪ ♪

00:43:01.578 --> 00:43:03.246
BRITTANY: Here we are.

00:43:03.246 --> 00:43:05.782
We were only in town
for about 24 hours.

00:43:05.782 --> 00:43:08.818
I definitely wish that
we could spend more time there.

00:43:08.818 --> 00:43:11.855
I don't know when
the next town trip will be.

00:43:11.855 --> 00:43:15.559
But this is really my home,
and I'm really glad to be home.

00:43:15.559 --> 00:43:16.927
Man, it's slick!

00:43:16.927 --> 00:43:19.329
Can we put some of those chains
on my feet?

00:43:19.329 --> 00:43:20.397
KALEB: Good to be home.

00:43:20.397 --> 00:43:22.332
(Brittany laughs)

00:43:22.332 --> 00:43:24.201
BRITTANY: Very good to be home.

00:43:27.103 --> 00:43:28.204
ALEX: If you're gonna
live out here,

00:43:28.204 --> 00:43:32.676
you have to kind of have
a mindset of a perpetual growth.

00:43:36.546 --> 00:43:38.081
I'll ask a question,
you know, like,

00:43:38.081 --> 00:43:40.450
if these trees had something
to teach me about life,

00:43:40.450 --> 00:43:42.686
like, what would they say?

00:43:42.686 --> 00:43:43.854
I think trees would tell you

00:43:43.854 --> 00:43:46.123
growth is the most
important thing.

00:43:46.123 --> 00:43:48.158
As long as you grow
a little bit,

00:43:48.158 --> 00:43:49.592
you're doing as good
as you can do,

00:43:49.592 --> 00:43:50.827
you know,
you're making progress.

00:43:50.827 --> 00:43:54.865
♪ ♪

00:43:54.865 --> 00:43:57.234
Today was a good day.

00:43:57.234 --> 00:44:00.437
 ♪ River song's gonna
 pull me through ♪

00:44:00.437 --> 00:44:03.406
 ♪ The rest you can't save ♪

00:44:03.406 --> 00:44:06.443
 ♪ Oh, my lord, take this soul ♪

00:44:06.443 --> 00:44:09.446
 ♪ Lay me at the bottom
 of the river ♪

